abstract:
We construct a Parrondo's game using discrete time quantum walks. Two lossing games are represented by two different coin operators. By mixing the two coin operators $U_{A}(α_{A},β_{A},γ_{A})$ and $U_{B}(α_{B},β_{B},γ_{B})$, we may win the game. Here we mix the two games in position instead of time. With a number of selections of the parameters, we can win the game with sequences ABB, ABBB, \emph{et al}. If we set $β_{A}=45^{\circ},γ_{A}=0,α_{B}=0,β_{B}=88^{\circ}$, we find the game 1\emph{}with {
ormalsize $U_{A}^{S}=U^{S}(-51^{\circ},45^{\circ},0)$, $U_{B}^{S}=U^{S}(0,88^{\circ},-16^{\circ})$ will win and get the most profit.}If we set $α_{A}=0,β_{A}=45^{\circ},α_{B}=0,β_{B}=88^{\circ}$ and{
